    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Ugashik’s name comes from the traditional Aleut word “Oogashik,” first recorded in 1880.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.alaskapeninsulacorp.com/land/ugashik/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Ugashik Narrows, where the two Ugashik Lakes rivers meet, form a shallow, narrow channel prized by sport fishers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.alaskapeninsulacorp.com/land/ugashik/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Ugashik’s salmon-rich waters once hosted an Alaska Packers cannery that operated until 1957.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ugashik%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Briggs Way Cannery, now Ugashik Wild Salmon Co., opened in 1962 and still processes fish.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ugashik%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The village is home to Aleut, Sugpiaq, and Yup’ik peoples who maintain traditional fishing and weaving practices.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.alaskapeninsulacorp.com/land/ugashik/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Ugashik sits on the east bank of the Ugashik River, 10 miles upriver from its mouth in Ugashik Bay.",
